gpui_windows: Popup passthrough and activation

Popups and dialogs are created at their requested bounds, kept inside the
work area, instead of at CW_USEDEFAULT and zero size, so their first frame
measurement sees their actual borders. A PopUp window is now WS_POPUP.

A popup opened with `WindowOptions::mouse_passthrough` forwards mouse hit
testing to the windows below it and never activates; a popup's
passthrough can change later without recreating it, and activate() does
nothing while it is on. A window without WS_EX_NOACTIVATE still activates
on click, and a no-activate one keeps its owner active.

A native activation handler runs inside WM_ACTIVATE, before GPUI's
deferred active-status observers, and is retired when it is replaced or
the window closes.

The window_lifecycle example drives these and the window controls of the
previous patch on a real desktop.

Newport needs it for Remote apps on Windows: Linux menus and tooltips are
popups, and shared focus between a remote window and its popups must
follow native activation before GPUI observes it.

set_native_activation_handler and set_popup_mouse_passthrough exist only
on Windows and have defaults. WindowOptions gains a
Windows-only mouse_passthrough field, so struct literals on Windows must
set it or use `..Default::default()`.

Remove when upstream GPUI has popup passthrough and a synchronous
activation hook on Windows.

